/* Verse Games: THE WHOLE COUNSEL · Song of Solomon chapters 1-5
   Verse text is filled/verified from the KJV by tools/fill-verses.js */
VG.registerBibleChapters("song", [
{n:1, name:"The Song of Songs",
 seal:{ref:"Song of Solomon 1:3",verse:"Because of the savour of thy good ointments thy name is as ointment poured forth, therefore do the virgins love thee."},
 facts:["Did you know 'the song of songs' is a Hebrew superlative, like 'king of kings', meaning the greatest song of all (1:1)?","Did you know the tents of Kedar were woven from dark goat hair, which is why the bride says she is dark 'as the tents of Kedar' (1:5)?","Did you know En-gedi was a real oasis on the western shore of the Dead Sea, famous for its fragrant balsam gardens (1:14)?"],
 questions:[
{q:"What does the opening verse call this book?", options:["The song of songs, which is Solomon's","The psalm of songs, which is David's","The proverb of love, which is Solomon's","The song of singers, which is Asaph's"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 1:1",verse:"The song of songs, which is Solomon’s.",
 insight:"The Bible itself names this the greatest of songs and sets Solomon's name on it; love at its best is worth singing about with all your heart."},
{q:"What does the bride say is better than wine?", options:["Thy love","Thy gold","Thy vineyards","Thy chariots"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 1:2",verse:"Let him kiss me with the kisses of his mouth: for thy love is better than wine.",
 insight:"She measures his love against the finest pleasure she knows, and love wins; true love outlasts and outshines every passing delight."},
{q:"Who speaks the words 'Let him kiss me with the kisses of his mouth'?", options:["The bride, longing for her beloved","The king, addressing his court","The daughters of Jerusalem, teasing the bride","The watchmen, calling through the city"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 1:2",verse:"Let him kiss me with the kisses of his mouth: for thy love is better than wine.",
 insight:"Her longing is open and unashamed; in a pure love there is no shame in wanting to be near the one you love."},
{q:"Because of the savour of his good ointments, what is his name like?", options:["As ointment poured forth","As wine kept long in the cellar","As gold hidden in a chest","As honey sealed in the comb"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 1:3",verse:"Because of the savour of thy good ointments thy name is as ointment poured forth, therefore do the virgins love thee.",
 insight:"A good name spreads like perfume, felt before it is seen; a life of kindness leaves a fragrance that others carry with them."},
{q:"Who has brought the bride into his chambers?", options:["The king","The chief shepherd","Her brother","The merchant of Lebanon"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 1:4",verse:"Draw me, we will run after thee: the king hath brought me into his chambers: we will be glad and rejoice in thee, we will remember thy love more than wine: the upright love thee.",
 insight:"She is not wandering; she has been brought in, welcomed and kept; belonging is at the heart of every true love."},
{q:"How does the bride describe herself to the daughters of Jerusalem?", options:["I am black, but comely","I am fair, but weary","I am young, but wise","I am lowly, but rich"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 1:5",verse:"I am black, but comely, O ye daughters of Jerusalem, as the tents of Kedar, as the curtains of Solomon.",
 insight:"She names her difference and her beauty in one breath; you can be honest about what sets you apart and still know your worth."},
{q:"Why does the bride ask them not to look upon her?", options:["Because the sun hath looked upon me","Because she wears the clothes of mourning","Because the king commanded it","Because her eyes are full of tears"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 1:6",verse:"Look not upon me, because I am black, because the sun hath looked upon me: my mother’s children were angry with me; they made me the keeper of the vineyards; but mine own vineyard have I not kept.",
 insight:"Hard work under the sun had darkened her skin; honest labor leaves its marks, and she bears hers without hiding."},
{q:"Whose children made the bride the keeper of the vineyards?", options:["Her mother's children","Her father's children","The king's children","The shepherd's children"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 1:6",verse:"Look not upon me, because I am black, because the sun hath looked upon me: my mother’s children were angry with me; they made me the keeper of the vineyards; but mine own vineyard have I not kept.",
 insight:"Her own brothers set her to work in the fields; family can be the first to overlook you, yet she kept serving faithfully."},
{q:"What two things does the bride want her beloved to tell her?", options:["Where he feeds, and where he makes his flock rest at noon","When he will marry her, and where they will live","How many sheep he owns, and where he bought them","Why he left her, and when he will return"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 1:7",verse:"Tell me, O thou whom my soul loveth, where thou feedest, where thou makest thy flock to rest at noon: for why should I be as one that turneth aside by the flocks of thy companions?",
 insight:"She wants to be where he is, even in the ordinary hours of the day; love delights to share the common hours, not only the grand ones."},
{q:"At what time of day does the beloved make his flock to rest?", options:["At noon","At sunset","At midnight","At dawn"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 1:7",verse:"Tell me, O thou whom my soul loveth, where thou feedest, where thou makest thy flock to rest at noon: for why should I be as one that turneth aside by the flocks of thy companions?",
 insight:"Even shepherds rest at midday; the busiest life needs its pauses, and love makes room for rest."},
{q:"Where is the bride told to feed her kids?", options:["Beside the shepherds' tents","In the king's palace courts","By the vineyards of En-gedi","In the streets of Jerusalem"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 1:8",verse:"If thou know not, O thou fairest among women, go thy way forth by the footsteps of the flock, and feed thy kids beside the shepherds’ tents.",
 insight:"She is guided to the well worn path where the shepherds already go; when you do not know the way, follow those who have walked it before you."},
{q:"To what does the beloved compare her?", options:["A company of horses in Pharaoh's chariots","A flock of doves in the temple courts","A garden of lilies by the river","A tower of ivory upon the wall"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 1:9",verse:"I have compared thee, O my love, to a company of horses in Pharaoh’s chariots.",
 insight:"Chariot horses were the finest, most spirited creatures of that age; he sees strength and nobility in her, not only gentleness."},
{q:"What makes her cheeks comely and her neck beautiful?", options:["Rows of jewels and chains of gold","Roses of Sharon and lilies of the valley","Clusters of grapes and bands of silver","Threads of scarlet and cords of purple"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 1:10",verse:"Thy cheeks are comely with rows of jewels, thy neck with chains of gold.",
 insight:"He notices the details and delights in them; loving attention is one of the truest forms of honor."},
{q:"What do they promise to make for her?", options:["Borders of gold with studs of silver","Cups of silver with rims of gold","A crown of gold with precious stones","Sandals of leather with silver buckles"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 1:11",verse:"We will make thee borders of gold with studs of silver.",
 insight:"Love is generous and creative, always thinking of how to add beauty; giving is the natural language of a glad heart."},
{q:"While the king sits at his table, what sends forth its smell?", options:["Her spikenard","His cup of wine","The myrrh on his robe","The lilies in the hall"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 1:12",verse:"While the king sitteth at his table, my spikenard sendeth forth the smell thereof.",
 insight:"Her presence fills the room like perfume; a quiet, gracious spirit is noticed even when it says nothing."},
{q:"What is her wellbeloved unto her?", options:["A bundle of myrrh","A cluster of ripe grapes","A vial of precious oil","A sheaf of golden wheat"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 1:13",verse:"A bundle of myrrh is my wellbeloved unto me; he shall lie all night betwixt my breasts.",
 insight:"Myrrh was precious and lasting; she keeps him close to her heart, the way we hold what we treasure most."},
{q:"Her beloved is as a cluster of camphire in whose vineyards?", options:["The vineyards of En-gedi","The vineyards of Hebron","The vineyards of Sharon","The vineyards of Gilead"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 1:14",verse:"My beloved is unto me as a cluster of camphire in the vineyards of En-gedi.",
 insight:"En-gedi was an oasis in the desert; the best loves are like water in a dry place, refreshment where you least expect it."},
{q:"What are her eyes compared to?", options:["Doves' eyes","The eyes of a young hart","Apples of gold in silver","Stars of the morning sky"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 1:15",verse:"Behold, thou art fair, my love; behold, thou art fair; thou hast doves’ eyes.",
 insight:"Doves are gentle and faithful; he praises not only how she looks, but the tenderness he sees looking back at him."},
{q:"How is their bed described?", options:["Green","Covered in purple","Made of cedar","Strewn with lilies"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 1:16",verse:"Behold, thou art fair, my beloved, yea, pleasant: also our bed is green.",
 insight:"Green speaks of life and freshness; love that stays fresh is love that keeps growing, like a living field."},
{q:"What are the beams of their house, and the rafters?", options:["Beams of cedar, rafters of fir","Beams of oak, rafters of cedar","Beams of fir, rafters of olive","Beams of cedar, rafters of cypress"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 1:17",verse:"The beams of our house are cedar, and our rafters of fir.",
 insight:"Cedar and fir were the strongest timbers of Lebanon; a love built on lasting things stands like a house of cedar."}
]},
{n:2, name:"The Banqueting House",
 seal:{ref:"Song of Solomon 2:16",verse:"My beloved is mine, and I am his: he feedeth among the lilies."},
 facts:["Did you know the 'turtle' whose voice is heard in the land (2:12) is the turtledove, whose spring song told farmers that winter was over?","Did you know the rose of Sharon (2:1) grew on the fertile coastal plain west of Jerusalem, a place known for its wildflowers?","Did you know little foxes really did spoil vineyards in ancient Israel by digging under the young vines, which is why the tender grapes needed guarding (2:15)?"],
 questions:[
{q:"Who says, 'I am the rose of Sharon, and the lily of the valleys'?", options:["The bride","The king","The daughters of Jerusalem","The watchmen"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 2:1",verse:"I am the rose of Sharon, and the lily of the valleys.",
 insight:"She compares herself to common wildflowers, not palace roses; there is a quiet humility in calling yourself a field flower."},
{q:"As the lily among thorns, so is my love among whom?", options:["Among the daughters","Among the sons","Among the shepherds","Among the queens"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 2:2",verse:"As the lily among thorns, so is my love among the daughters.",
 insight:"He lifts her above all comparison; in his eyes she stands out like a single lily in a thorn patch."},
{q:"What was sweet to her taste as she sat under his shadow?", options:["His fruit","The apples of Sharon","The honey of the comb","The wine of the banquet"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 2:3",verse:"As the apple tree among the trees of the wood, so is my beloved among the sons. I sat down under his shadow with great delight, and his fruit was sweet to my taste.",
 insight:"She finds rest in his shade and sweetness in his fruit; a good love is both shelter and nourishment."},
{q:"Where did he bring her, and what was his banner over her?", options:["To the banqueting house, and his banner over me was love","To the king's garden, and his banner over me was gold","To the house of wine, and his banner over me was joy","To the sheepfold, and his banner over me was peace"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 2:4",verse:"He brought me to the banqueting house, and his banner over me was love.",
 insight:"Over all the feasting flies one banner: love; where love rules, every table becomes a banquet."},
{q:"With what does she ask to be stayed and comforted?", options:["With flagons, and with apples","With wine, and with bread","With music, and with dancing","With myrrh, and with spices"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 2:5",verse:"Stay me with flagons, comfort me with apples: for I am sick of love.",
 insight:"She is so full of love she can hardly bear it; even good things can overwhelm us, and it is honest to say so."},
{q:"Why does she need staying and comforting?", options:["For she is sick of love","For she is weary from the journey","For she is faint with hunger","For she is troubled by a dream"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 2:5",verse:"Stay me with flagons, comfort me with apples: for I am sick of love.",
 insight:"'Sick of love' is lovesickness, the ache of a heart too full; strong feeling is not weakness, it is a sign of how deeply we were made to love."},
{q:"How does he hold her?", options:["His left hand under her head, his right hand embracing her","His right hand under her head, his left hand holding hers","Both his arms around her shoulders","His hand upon her brow, blessing her"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 2:6",verse:"His left hand is under my head, and his right hand doth embrace me.",
 insight:"She is held securely, head cradled and embraced; true love makes the beloved feel safe, not grasped."},
{q:"By what does she charge the daughters of Jerusalem?", options:["By the roes, and by the hinds of the field","By the doves of the valleys","By the lilies of Sharon","By the stars of heaven"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 2:7",verse:"I charge you, O ye daughters of Jerusalem, by the roes, and by the hinds of the field, that ye stir not up, nor awake my love, till he please.",
 insight:"She swears by the gentle wild creatures; love is not to be forced or hurried, and she guards its proper time."},
{q:"How does her beloved come to her?", options:["Leaping upon the mountains, skipping upon the hills","Riding upon horses through the valley","Walking slowly beside the still waters","Sailing in ships upon the sea"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 2:8",verse:"The voice of my beloved! behold, he cometh leaping upon the mountains, skipping upon the hills.",
 insight:"He comes with joy and eagerness, not dragging his feet; the one who loves you hurries to be near you."},
{q:"What is her beloved like, and where does he stand?", options:["Like a roe or a young hart, standing behind our wall","Like a lion of Judah, standing at the city gate","Like an eagle of the rocks, standing on the tower","Like a lamb of the flock, standing in the doorway"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 2:9",verse:"My beloved is like a roe or a young hart: behold, he standeth behind our wall, he looketh forth at the windows, shewing himself through the lattice.",
 insight:"He is near, just behind the wall, looking through the lattice; love draws close and waits to be welcomed, never breaking down the door."},
{q:"What does her beloved say to her?", options:["Rise up, my love, my fair one, and come away","Stay here, my love, and rest a while","Go back, my love, the night is falling","Wait for me, my love, till morning comes"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 2:10",verse:"My beloved spake, and said unto me, Rise up, my love, my fair one, and come away.",
 insight:"He calls her to come away with him; love is an invitation, always beckoning us out into something wider."},
{q:"What has happened, as a sign the time has come?", options:["The winter is past, the rain is over and gone","The summer is come, the heat is upon the land","The harvest is past, the barns are full","The spring is delayed, the cold remains"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 2:11",verse:"For, lo, the winter is past, the rain is over and gone;",
 insight:"Winter always passes; every season of waiting has an end, and love knows when the new season has begun."},
{q:"What is the 'turtle' whose voice is heard in the land?", options:["The turtledove","The tortoise","The sea turtle","A small hand drum"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 2:12",verse:"The flowers appear on the earth; the time of the singing of birds is come, and the voice of the turtle is heard in our land;",
 insight:"The turtledove's spring song announced winter's end; God fills creation with small voices that tell us hope is returning."},
{q:"What appears on the earth?", options:["The flowers","The first rains","The flocks","The tents"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 2:12",verse:"The flowers appear on the earth; the time of the singing of birds is come, and the voice of the turtle is heard in our land;",
 insight:"Flowers are the earth's quiet celebration; new life has a way of showing itself, and joy loves to bloom in the open."},
{q:"What does the fig tree put forth?", options:["Her green figs","Her broad leaves","Her sweet blossoms","Her tender branches"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 2:13",verse:"The fig tree putteth forth her green figs, and the vines with the tender grape give a good smell. Arise, my love, my fair one, and come away.",
 insight:"The fig tree keeps its own season and bears in its time; growth cannot be rushed, and fruit comes to those who wait for ripeness."},
{q:"Where is his dove, whom he calls to?", options:["In the clefts of the rock, in the secret places of the stairs","In the branches of the cedars of Lebanon","In the courts of the king's palace","In the shade of the apple trees"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 2:14",verse:"O my dove, that art in the clefts of the rock, in the secret places of the stairs, let me see thy countenance, let me hear thy voice; for sweet is thy voice, and thy countenance is comely.",
 insight:"She hides like a dove in the rocks; love does not drag us out of hiding but calls gently until we are ready to be seen."},
{q:"What does he say is sweet, and what is comely?", options:["Sweet is thy voice, and thy countenance is comely","Sweet is thy song, and thy garments are comely","Sweet is thy name, and thy dwelling is comely","Sweet is thy smile, and thy hands are comely"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 2:14",verse:"O my dove, that art in the clefts of the rock, in the secret places of the stairs, let me see thy countenance, let me hear thy voice; for sweet is thy voice, and thy countenance is comely.",
 insight:"He wants to hear her voice and see her face; to be truly loved is to be truly listened to."},
{q:"What must be caught, because they spoil the vines?", options:["The foxes, the little foxes","The locusts, the little locusts","The birds, the little birds","The worms, the little worms"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 2:15",verse:"Take us the foxes, the little foxes, that spoil the vines: for our vines have tender grapes.",
 insight:"It is the little foxes that spoil the vines; small neglects, not great disasters, most often damage a tender love."},
{q:"What does the bride declare about her beloved?", options:["My beloved is mine, and I am his","My beloved is gone, and I am alone","My beloved is far, and I am waiting","My beloved is king, and I am his servant"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 2:16",verse:"My beloved is mine, and I am his: he feedeth among the lilies.",
 insight:"Mutual belonging, simply stated; the deepest covenant is this quiet certainty: I am his, and he is mine."},
{q:"Until when does she ask him to turn and be like a roe on the mountains of Bether?", options:["Until the day break, and the shadows flee away","Until the evening fall, and the stars appear","Until the winter pass, and the spring return","Until the moon rise, and the night be spent"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 2:17",verse:"Until the day break, and the shadows flee away, turn, my beloved, and be thou like a roe or a young hart upon the mountains of Bether.",
 insight:"She longs for the daybreak when shadows flee; every love longs for the morning when all hiding ends."}
]},
{n:3, name:"Sought and Found",
 seal:{ref:"Song of Solomon 3:11",verse:"Go forth, O ye daughters of Zion, and behold king Solomon with the crown wherewith his mother crowned him in the day of his espousals, and in the day of the gladness of his heart."},
 facts:["Did you know 'threescore' (3:7) is the old word for sixty, so sixty armed guards surrounded Solomon's traveling bed?","Did you know myrrh and frankincense (3:6) were costly imported resins from Arabia and beyond, burned to honor the approaching king?","Did you know the 'day of his espousals' (3:11) is the wedding day itself, and the crown was set on Solomon by his own mother?"],
 questions:[
{q:"When did she seek him whom her soul loveth?", options:["By night on my bed","At dawn in the garden","At noon by the flocks","At evening on the wall"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 3:1",verse:"By night on my bed I sought him whom my soul loveth: I sought him, but I found him not.",
 insight:"Her longing keeps her awake; the heart that loves does not clock out when the day ends."},
{q:"What was the result of her seeking?", options:["She sought him, but found him not","She found him at once","She dreamed of him instead","She gave up and slept"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 3:1",verse:"By night on my bed I sought him whom my soul loveth: I sought him, but I found him not.",
 insight:"Seeking does not always find at once; the night of not finding is part of learning how much he matters."},
{q:"What did she resolve to do?", options:["Rise and go about the city in the streets and broad ways","Send messengers to every gate","Wait in her chamber till morning","Climb to the top of the wall"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 3:2",verse:"I will rise now, and go about the city in the streets, and in the broad ways I will seek him whom my soul loveth: I sought him, but I found him not.",
 insight:"She does not wait for love to come to her; when he is missing, she gets up and goes looking."},
{q:"Where did she look for him?", options:["In the streets, and in the broad ways of the city","In the king's palace and the temple courts","In the vineyards and the olive groves","In the marketplace among the merchants"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 3:2",verse:"I will rise now, and go about the city in the streets, and in the broad ways I will seek him whom my soul loveth: I sought him, but I found him not.",
 insight:"She searches the public places, unafraid to be seen looking; real longing is not embarrassed to ask and seek."},
{q:"Who found her as she searched the city?", options:["The watchmen that go about the city","The daughters of Jerusalem","The shepherds of the flocks","The servants of the king"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 3:3",verse:"The watchmen that go about the city found me: to whom I said, Saw ye him whom my soul loveth?",
 insight:"The night watch found her wandering; even our loneliest searches happen under the eyes of others."},
{q:"What did she ask the watchmen?", options:["Saw ye him whom my soul loveth?","Where is the king's palace?","Have the gates been shut?","Is the night far spent?"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 3:3",verse:"The watchmen that go about the city found me: to whom I said, Saw ye him whom my soul loveth?",
 insight:"She asks strangers about her beloved; when love fills your heart, it becomes the one question on your lips."},
{q:"How soon after passing the watchmen did she find him?", options:["It was but a little that she passed from them","Not until the morning light","After seven days of searching","Only when the watchmen brought him"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 3:4",verse:"It was but a little that I passed from them, but I found him whom my soul loveth: I held him, and would not let him go, until I had brought him into my mother’s house, and into the chamber of her that conceived me.",
 insight:"She found him just beyond the place of asking; sometimes the answer is nearer than our searching makes it feel."},
{q:"Where did she bring him when she found him?", options:["Into her mother's house, into the chamber of her that conceived her","Into the king's banqueting house","Into the garden of spices","Into the shepherds' tents"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 3:4",verse:"It was but a little that I passed from them, but I found him whom my soul loveth: I held him, and would not let him go, until I had brought him into my mother’s house, and into the chamber of her that conceived me.",
 insight:"She brings him home to her mother's house; love that is real wants the blessing of family, not secrecy."},
{q:"By what does she charge the daughters of Jerusalem?", options:["By the roes, and by the hinds of the field","By the crown of the king","By the walls of the city","By the oath of her mother"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 3:5",verse:"I charge you, O ye daughters of Jerusalem, by the roes, and by the hinds of the field, that ye stir not up, nor awake my love, till he please.",
 insight:"Again she guards love's timing with the gentlest of oaths; what is precious should never be forced awake before its hour."},
{q:"What does she charge them not to do?", options:["Stir not up, nor awake my love, till he please","Speak not of my beloved to strangers","Go not out by night into the streets","Weep not for the winter that is past"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 3:5",verse:"I charge you, O ye daughters of Jerusalem, by the roes, and by the hinds of the field, that ye stir not up, nor awake my love, till he please.",
 insight:"Love has its own right time; patience protects what haste would spoil."},
{q:"What does the coming one look like?", options:["Like pillars of smoke","Like a cloud of dust","Like a flock of doves","Like a river of gold"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 3:6",verse:"Who is this that cometh out of the wilderness like pillars of smoke, perfumed with myrrh and frankincense, with all powders of the merchant?",
 insight:"Pillars of smoke rise high and are seen from far; true majesty does not need to announce itself loudly to be noticed."},
{q:"With what is the coming one perfumed?", options:["With myrrh and frankincense, with all powders of the merchant","With spikenard and saffron from the gardens","With oil of olives and wine of the press","With roses of Sharon and lilies of the valley"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 3:6",verse:"Who is this that cometh out of the wilderness like pillars of smoke, perfumed with myrrh and frankincense, with all powders of the merchant?",
 insight:"The king's approach is announced by fragrance; honor goes before greatness, and a good name perfumes the air around it."},
{q:"How many valiant men are about Solomon's bed?", options:["Threescore","Thirty","Twelve","A hundred"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 3:7",verse:"Behold his bed, which is Solomon’s; threescore valiant men are about it, of the valiant of Israel.",
 insight:"Sixty armed men guard the king's rest; even kings need watching in the night, and God gives His beloved sleep."},
{q:"Of whom are these valiant men?", options:["Of the valiant of Israel","Of the mighty men of Egypt","Of the captains of Lebanon","Of the guards of the temple"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 3:7",verse:"Behold his bed, which is Solomon’s; threescore valiant men are about it, of the valiant of Israel.",
 insight:"Israel's bravest surround their king; strength is meant to serve and protect, not to threaten."},
{q:"Why does every man have his sword upon his thigh?", options:["Because of fear in the night","Because the king commanded a parade","Because war had been declared","Because thieves were in the city"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 3:8",verse:"They all hold swords, being expert in war: every man hath his sword upon his thigh because of fear in the night.",
 insight:"Even celebration has its watchmen; wisdom enjoys the feast while keeping one eye on the night."},
{q:"Who made himself a chariot?", options:["King Solomon","King David","The prince of Lebanon","The captain of the guard"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 3:9",verse:"King Solomon made himself a chariot of the wood of Lebanon.",
 insight:"Solomon builds his own wedding chariot; love takes personal care, and does not leave the preparations to others."},
{q:"Of what wood was the chariot made?", options:["The wood of Lebanon","The wood of Gilead","The wood of Sharon","The wood of Carmel"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 3:9",verse:"King Solomon made himself a chariot of the wood of Lebanon.",
 insight:"Lebanon's cedars were the pride of the ancient world; what we build for love should be built to last."},
{q:"What was the covering of the chariot?", options:["Of purple","Of scarlet","Of blue","Of white linen"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 3:10",verse:"He made the pillars thereof of silver, the bottom thereof of gold, the covering of it of purple, the midst thereof being paved with love, for the daughters of Jerusalem.",
 insight:"Purple was the color of royalty; when love is honored, everything around it is lifted up."},
{q:"With what crown is Solomon shown?", options:["The crown wherewith his mother crowned him","The crown of the kingdom of Israel","The crown of gold from Egypt","The crown of his father David"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 3:11",verse:"Go forth, O ye daughters of Zion, and behold king Solomon with the crown wherewith his mother crowned him in the day of his espousals, and in the day of the gladness of his heart.",
 insight:"His mother crowns him on his wedding day; a mother's blessing on a marriage is a crown no gold can match."},
{q:"On what day did his mother crown him?", options:["In the day of his espousals, and in the day of the gladness of his heart","In the day he took the throne of David","In the day of the great Passover feast","In the day he finished the temple"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 3:11",verse:"Go forth, O ye daughters of Zion, and behold king Solomon with the crown wherewith his mother crowned him in the day of his espousals, and in the day of the gladness of his heart.",
 insight:"The wedding day is called the gladness of his heart; Scripture itself calls marriage a joy, not a burden."}
]},
{n:4, name:"Thou Art All Fair",
 seal:{ref:"Song of Solomon 4:7",verse:"Thou art all fair, my love; there is no spot in thee."},
 facts:["Did you know the goats of Mount Gilead had long dark hair, so a flock moving down the hillside really did look like flowing hair (4:1)?","Did you know the tower of David (4:4) was a real fortress in Jerusalem, its walls hung with captured shields as trophies of victory?","Did you know saffron, cinnamon, and aloes (4:14) were luxury imports in Solomon's day, so the garden list reads like a royal treasure inventory?"],
 questions:[
{q:"What are her eyes compared to?", options:["Doves' eyes within her locks","The eyes of a young hart","Apples of gold in pictures of silver","Stars shining in the night"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 4:1",verse:"Behold, thou art fair, my love; behold, thou art fair; thou hast doves’ eyes within thy locks: thy hair is as a flock of goats, that appear from mount Gilead.",
 insight:"Doves are gentle and true; he sees a tenderness in her gaze that no jewel could match."},
{q:"What is her hair like?", options:["As a flock of goats from mount Gilead","As a field of wheat in the harvest","As the wings of the morning","As the fleece of white sheep"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 4:1",verse:"Behold, thou art fair, my love; behold, thou art fair; thou hast doves’ eyes within thy locks: thy hair is as a flock of goats, that appear from mount Gilead.",
 insight:"A dark flock flowing down Gilead's slopes was a sight of beauty; he finds poetry in the everyday world around them."},
{q:"What are her teeth like?", options:["Like a flock of sheep, even shorn, come up from the washing","Like pearls set in a band of gold","Like white doves by the rivers of water","Like ivory keys on a polished board"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 4:2",verse:"Thy teeth are like a flock of sheep that are even shorn, which came up from the washing; whereof every one bear twins, and none is barren among them.",
 insight:"Clean, whole, every one bearing twins, none barren; he admires completeness and health, the beauty of a life in good order."},
{q:"What are her lips like?", options:["Like a thread of scarlet","Like a cluster of ripe grapes","Like the petals of a rose","Like a cord of purple"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 4:3",verse:"Thy lips are like a thread of scarlet, and thy speech is comely: thy temples are like a piece of a pomegranate within thy locks.",
 insight:"A single scarlet thread, fine and bright; sometimes the smallest detail carries the greatest beauty."},
{q:"What are her temples like?", options:["Like a piece of a pomegranate within her locks","Like the half of an apple in the orchard","Like a slice of citron in the garden","Like a wedge of honeycomb in the hive"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 4:3",verse:"Thy lips are like a thread of scarlet, and thy speech is comely: thy temples are like a piece of a pomegranate within thy locks.",
 insight:"The pomegranate's blush shows through her hair; he notices the quiet colors, the beauty that does not shout."},
{q:"What is her neck like, and what hangs on it?", options:["Like the tower of David, with a thousand bucklers hanging on it","Like a pillar of marble, with chains of gold","Like the wall of Jerusalem, with shields of brass","Like a column of ivory, with strings of pearls"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 4:4",verse:"Thy neck is like the tower of David builded for an armoury, whereon there hang a thousand bucklers, all shields of mighty men.",
 insight:"The tower of David displayed the shields of mighty men; he sees dignity and strength in her bearing, a neck held high."},
{q:"To what are her two breasts compared?", options:["Two young roes that are twins, feeding among the lilies","Two doves resting in the clefts of the rock","Two apples hanging in the orchard","Two fawns playing in the meadow"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 4:5",verse:"Thy two breasts are like two young roes that are twins, which feed among the lilies.",
 insight:"Young roes are graceful and lively; his praise stays within the garden of modest, natural imagery."},
{q:"Where will he go until the day break and the shadows flee?", options:["To the mountain of myrrh, and the hill of frankincense","To the valleys of Sharon among the lilies","To the vineyards of En-gedi by the sea","To the top of Lebanon among the cedars"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 4:6",verse:"Until the day break, and the shadows flee away, I will get me to the mountain of myrrh, and to the hill of frankincense.",
 insight:"He withdraws to the fragrant hills to wait for morning; love knows how to wait in a sweet place until the shadows flee."},
{q:"What does he declare about her?", options:["Thou art all fair, my love; there is no spot in thee","Thou art wise, my love; there is no fault in thee","Thou art rich, my love; there is no want in thee","Thou art strong, my love; there is no fear in thee"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 4:7",verse:"Thou art all fair, my love; there is no spot in thee.",
 insight:"All fair, no spot; this is how love sees at its best, and it is how Christ sees His church, washed clean."},
{q:"From where does he call her to come with him?", options:["From Lebanon","From Egypt","From Sharon","From Gilead"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 4:8",verse:"Come with me from Lebanon, my spouse, with me from Lebanon: look from the top of Amana, from the top of Shenir and Hermon, from the lions’ dens, from the mountains of the leopards.",
 insight:"He calls her down from the high, wild places; love invites us out of the lonely heights and into company."},
{q:"From what dangerous places does he call her to look away?", options:["From the lions' dens, from the mountains of the leopards","From the pits of the valley, from the snares of the fowler","From the depths of the sea, from the caves of the bears","From the deserts of the south, from the hills of the wolves"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 4:8",verse:"Come with me from Lebanon, my spouse, with me from Lebanon: look from the top of Amana, from the top of Shenir and Hermon, from the lions’ dens, from the mountains of the leopards.",
 insight:"He names the real dangers and calls her away from them; love does not pretend the lions are not there, it leads us safely past them."},
{q:"With what has she ravished his heart?", options:["With one of her eyes, with one chain of her neck","With her songs in the night, with her dancing","With her words of wisdom, with her counsel","With her gifts of gold, with her spices"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 4:9",verse:"Thou hast ravished my heart, my sister, my spouse; thou hast ravished my heart with one of thine eyes, with one chain of thy neck.",
 insight:"One glance, one small ornament, and his heart is captured; love is moved by little things, not only by grand gestures."},
{q:"What does he say is better than wine?", options:["Thy love","Thy beauty","Thy wisdom","Thy dowry"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 4:10",verse:"How fair is thy love, my sister, my spouse! how much better is thy love than wine! and the smell of thine ointments than all spices!",
 insight:"Again love outshines wine; the finest earthly pleasures are only shadows of what love itself gives."},
{q:"What is under her tongue?", options:["Honey and milk","Myrrh and frankincense","Wine and oil","Manna and honey"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 4:11",verse:"Thy lips, O my spouse, drop as the honeycomb: honey and milk are under thy tongue; and the smell of thy garments is like the smell of Lebanon.",
 insight:"Honey and milk under the tongue; her words nourish and sweeten, the way kind speech feeds everyone who hears it."},
{q:"What three pictures describe her in verse 12?", options:["A garden inclosed, a spring shut up, a fountain sealed","A city walled, a gate locked, a tower guarded","A field fenced, a well covered, a vineyard hedged","A palace closed, a chamber barred, a treasure hidden"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 4:12",verse:"A garden inclosed is my sister, my spouse; a spring shut up, a fountain sealed.",
 insight:"Garden, spring, fountain, all kept and guarded; what is precious is protected, and purity is a treasure worth enclosing."},
{q:"What are her plants?", options:["An orchard of pomegranates, with pleasant fruits","A vineyard of choice vines, with sweet grapes","A grove of olives, with rich oil","A field of wheat, with golden grain"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 4:13",verse:"Thy plants are an orchard of pomegranates, with pleasant fruits; camphire, with spikenard,",
 insight:"An orchard, not a single flower; love grows many good things together, a whole garden of delights."},
{q:"Which two spices are named with the pleasant fruits?", options:["Camphire, with spikenard","Saffron, with cinnamon","Myrrh, with aloes","Calamus, with frankincense"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 4:13",verse:"Thy plants are an orchard of pomegranates, with pleasant fruits; camphire, with spikenard,",
 insight:"Camphire and spikenard were prized perfumes; the garden of love is planted with the rarest and best."},
{q:"Which spices are named in verse 14?", options:["Spikenard and saffron, calamus and cinnamon, frankincense, myrrh, and aloes","Balm and cassia, onycha and galbanum, stacte and myrrh","Rosemary and thyme, mint and rue, hyssop and marjoram","Coriander and cumin, anise and dill, mustard and rue"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 4:14",verse:"Spikenard and saffron; calamus and cinnamon, with all trees of frankincense; myrrh and aloes, with all the chief spices:",
 insight:"Seven precious spices in one verse; Scripture heaps up riches to say that love's garden holds nothing common or cheap."},
{q:"What is she called in verse 15?", options:["A fountain of gardens, a well of living waters, and streams from Lebanon","A river of delights, a pool of still waters, and rains from heaven","A cistern of cool water, a brook in the desert, and dew from Hermon","A spring in the valley, a lake among the hills, and mist from the sea"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 4:15",verse:"A fountain of gardens, a well of living waters, and streams from Lebanon.",
 insight:"Living water, not a stagnant pool; love that is fresh and flowing refreshes everyone downstream."},
{q:"Which winds does she call to blow upon her garden?", options:["The north wind to awake, and the south wind to come","The east wind to rise, and the west wind to fall","The north wind to cease, and the south wind to rest","The morning breeze to stir, and the evening breeze to cool"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 4:16",verse:"Awake, O north wind; and come, thou south; blow upon my garden, that the spices thereof may flow out. Let my beloved come into his garden, and eat his pleasant fruits.",
 insight:"She invites every wind to stir the spices; a heart in love welcomes all of God's seasons, asking only that His fragrance spread."}
]},
{n:5, name:"My Beloved, My Friend",
 seal:{ref:"Song of Solomon 5:16",verse:"His mouth is most sweet: yea, he is altogether lovely. This is my beloved, and this is my friend, O daughters of Jerusalem."},
 facts:["Did you know 'the chiefest among ten thousand' (5:10) means he stands out first in an army sized crowd, the one you would pick out at once?","Did you know the cedars of Lebanon (5:15) were the tallest and most prized trees of the ancient world, a picture of towering stateliness?","Did you know myrrh on the door handles (5:5) was a love token, and in the ancient world myrrh was worth nearly its weight in gold?"],
 questions:[
{q:"What does he bid his friends to do in his garden?", options:["Eat, O friends; drink, yea, drink abundantly, O beloved","Sing, O friends; dance, yea, dance joyfully, O beloved","Rest, O friends; sleep, yea, sleep sweetly, O beloved","Come, O friends; see, yea, see fully, O beloved"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 5:1",verse:"I am come into my garden, my sister, my spouse: I have gathered my myrrh with my spice; I have eaten my honeycomb with my honey; I have drunk my wine with my milk: eat, O friends; drink, yea, drink abundantly, O beloved.",
 insight:"Love throws the doors open and shares its joy; happiness kept to yourself is only half a feast."},
{q:"Whose voice is knocking at the door?", options:["The voice of her beloved","The voice of the watchmen","The voice of her mother","The voice of the daughters"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 5:2",verse:"I sleep, but my heart waketh: it is the voice of my beloved that knocketh, saying, Open to me, my sister, my love, my dove, my undefiled: for my head is filled with dew, and my locks with the drops of the night.",
 insight:"He comes knocking in the night, not forcing entry; even the most welcome love knocks and waits to be let in."},
{q:"What is on his head and his locks?", options:["Dew, and the drops of the night","Myrrh, and the oil of gladness","Dust, and the sand of the road","Flowers, and the dew of the morning"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 5:2",verse:"I sleep, but my heart waketh: it is the voice of my beloved that knocketh, saying, Open to me, my sister, my love, my dove, my undefiled: for my head is filled with dew, and my locks with the drops of the night.",
 insight:"He has waited long in the night damp; love that waits is love that costs something, and she will soon feel the weight of it."},
{q:"Why does she hesitate to open to him?", options:["She has put off her coat and washed her feet","She has barred the door and lost the key","She is asleep and cannot wake","She is angry and will not answer"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 5:3",verse:"I have put off my coat; how shall I put it on? I have washed my feet; how shall I defile them?",
 insight:"A small comfort keeps her from a great love; how often we let being settled keep us from answering the knock."},
{q:"When he puts his hand by the hole of the door, what happens within her?", options:["Her heart is moved for him","Her hands begin to tremble","Her voice fails her entirely","Her feet run to the window"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 5:4",verse:"My beloved put in his hand by the hole of the door, and my bowels were moved for him.",
 insight:"Something stirs deep within her at his nearness; the heart often knows before the mind catches up."},
{q:"What is on her hands when she rises to open?", options:["Myrrh, sweet smelling myrrh, upon the handles of the lock","Oil of olives upon the bolts of the door","Dew of the night upon the panels","Dust of the road upon the latch"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 5:5",verse:"I rose up to open to my beloved; and my hands dropped with myrrh, and my fingers with sweet smelling myrrh, upon the handles of the lock.",
 insight:"Her hands are fragrant with myrrh as she reaches for him; even her hesitation is perfumed with love."},
{q:"What does she find when she opens?", options:["Her beloved had withdrawn himself, and was gone","Her beloved stood waiting with gifts","The watchmen stood guarding the door","Her mother stood calling her name"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 5:6",verse:"I opened to my beloved; but my beloved had withdrawn himself, and was gone: my soul failed when he spake: I sought him, but I could not find him; I called him, but he gave me no answer.",
 insight:"She opens, but he is gone; love delayed can become love missed, and the moment does not always wait."},
{q:"What happens when she seeks and calls him?", options:["She sought him, but could not find him; she called, but he gave no answer","She found him at once in the garden","The watchmen brought him to her","Her mother answered in his place"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 5:6",verse:"I opened to my beloved; but my beloved had withdrawn himself, and was gone: my soul failed when he spake: I sought him, but I could not find him; I called him, but he gave me no answer.",
 insight:"Her own words now echo back unanswered; the silence teaches her what his voice was worth."},
{q:"What do the keepers of the walls take from her?", options:["Her veil","Her coat","Her jewels","Her sandals"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 5:7",verse:"The watchmen that went about the city found me, they smote me, they wounded me; the keepers of the walls took away my veil from me.",
 insight:"The night search costs her dignity and covering; wandering alone in the dark always exacts a price."},
{q:"What message does she send to her beloved by the daughters of Jerusalem?", options:["Tell him that I am sick of love","Tell him that I am weary of waiting","Tell him that I am angry at his leaving","Tell him that I am afraid of the night"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 5:8",verse:"I charge you, O daughters of Jerusalem, if ye find my beloved, that ye tell him, that I am sick of love.",
 insight:"She does not hide her lovesickness; an honest heart tells the truth about its ache instead of nursing it in silence."},
{q:"What do the daughters of Jerusalem ask her?", options:["What is thy beloved more than another beloved?","Where hast thou lost thy beloved?","Why dost thou weep for thy beloved?","When will thy beloved return?"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 5:9",verse:"What is thy beloved more than another beloved, O thou fairest among women? what is thy beloved more than another beloved, that thou dost so charge us?",
 insight:"Her longing makes them curious; a love that shows itself makes others want to understand it."},
{q:"How does she describe her beloved?", options:["White and ruddy, the chiefest among ten thousand","Tall and strong, the mightiest among thousands","Fair and gentle, the kindest among hundreds","Rich and noble, the greatest among princes"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 5:10",verse:"My beloved is white and ruddy, the chiefest among ten thousand.",
 insight:"White and ruddy, glowing with health, first among ten thousand; she sees him as distinct from every other, the one she would pick out of any crowd."},
{q:"What is his head like?", options:["As the most fine gold","As the polished bronze","As the carved ivory","As the white marble"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 5:11",verse:"His head is as the most fine gold, his locks are bushy, and black as a raven.",
 insight:"Gold is the most precious of metals; in her eyes there is nothing common about him, from head to foot."},
{q:"What are his locks like?", options:["Bushy, and black as a raven","Long, and white as wool","Curled, and red as flame","Braided, and dark as night"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 5:11",verse:"His head is as the most fine gold, his locks are bushy, and black as a raven.",
 insight:"Black as a raven, full and strong; she delights in his vigor, the picture of youthful strength."},
{q:"What are his eyes like?", options:["As doves by the rivers of waters, washed with milk, fitly set","As flames of fire in the dark night","As stars reflected in a still pool","As sapphires set in rings of gold"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 5:12",verse:"His eyes are as the eyes of doves by the rivers of waters, washed with milk, and fitly set.",
 insight:"Doves' eyes, washed with milk, perfectly set; she sees gentleness and clarity in his gaze, nothing harsh or shifting."},
{q:"What are his cheeks and lips like?", options:["Cheeks as a bed of spices, lips like lilies dropping myrrh","Cheeks as apples of gold, lips like threads of scarlet","Cheeks as the dawn sky, lips like petals of roses","Cheeks as polished ivory, lips like coral of the sea"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 5:13",verse:"His cheeks are as a bed of spices, as sweet flowers: his lips like lilies, dropping sweet smelling myrrh.",
 insight:"Spice beds and fragrant lilies; his very presence seems to sweeten the air around him."},
{q:"What are his hands and his belly like?", options:["Hands as gold rings set with beryl, belly as bright ivory overlaid with sapphires","Hands as rods of iron, belly as a shield of bronze","Hands as pillars of cedar, belly as a wall of marble","Hands as scepters of gold, belly as a breastplate of jewels"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 5:14",verse:"His hands are as gold rings set with the beryl: his belly is as bright ivory overlaid with sapphires.",
 insight:"Gold, beryl, ivory, sapphires; she reaches for the richest things she knows, and still feels they fall short of him."},
{q:"What are his legs and his countenance like?", options:["Legs as pillars of marble on sockets of fine gold, countenance as Lebanon, excellent as the cedars","Legs as columns of bronze on bases of silver, countenance as Carmel, fair as the vineyards","Legs as oaks of Bashan on foundations of stone, countenance as Hermon, white as the snow","Legs as towers of ivory on pedestals of alabaster, countenance as Zion, lovely as Jerusalem"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 5:15",verse:"His legs are as pillars of marble, set upon sockets of fine gold: his countenance is as Lebanon, excellent as the cedars.",
 insight:"Marble pillars and Lebanon's cedars speak of strength that does not bend; she trusts in a love that stands firm."},
{q:"What does she say of his mouth?", options:["His mouth is most sweet","His mouth is full of wisdom","His mouth is quick with laughter","His mouth is silent as the grave"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 5:16",verse:"His mouth is most sweet: yea, he is altogether lovely. This is my beloved, and this is my friend, O daughters of Jerusalem.",
 insight:"Most sweet; her highest praise is for his words, for what comes out of the mouth reveals the heart."},
{q:"How does she sum him up to the daughters of Jerusalem?", options:["This is my beloved, and this is my friend","This is my king, and this is my lord","This is my shepherd, and this is my guide","This is my master, and this is my keeper"], a:0, ref:"Song of Solomon 5:16",verse:"His mouth is most sweet: yea, he is altogether lovely. This is my beloved, and this is my friend, O daughters of Jerusalem.",
 insight:"Beloved and friend, both at once; the happiest loves join passion to friendship, and she claims both names for him."}
]}
]);
